<p>I spent several hours yesterday trying to get Wifi to remain up on the Framework after installing CachyOS. It was not easy. I had to drag Claude Code in to help. I'd have never been able to find and fix it, otherwise.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>Flaky Wifi is a commonly-reported problem with the Framework 13. In my case it was related to the Orbi mesh, so I don't know if this helps anyone else, but I'll leave it here, just in case. It's working for me.</p>
<p>TL;DR is pin wifi to a 5 GHz BSSID so the 6 GHz band doesn't get in the way.</p>
<p><strong>Here's a slightly edited version of Claude Code's summary:</strong></p>
<p><em>The fault.</em> The Framework connects, gets a DHCP lease, and stops passing
traffic about two minutes later. The link never drops — =iw= says =Connected=
throughout, the AP keeps acking frames, signal is fine. What dies is ARP:
<code>ping</code> starts returning <em>Destination Host Unreachable from the machine's own
address</em>, which is the kernel giving up after its ARP entry expires at ~60s and
cannot be refreshed. The &quot;two minutes&quot; is the ARP cache lifetime, not a
coincidence — and that is why every log looked healthy.</p>
<p><em>The cause.</em> The Orbi's 6 GHz radio is the mesh backhaul. It accepts a client
association and acknowledges frames but does not bridge that client to the LAN.
The MT7925 is Wi-Fi 7 and can see 6 GHz, so it sometimes anchors there and is
then deaf. Every Wi-Fi 5/6 device in the house is immune because it cannot see
the band — which is exactly why this ThinkPad sat on the <em>same AP</em> all day
without a hiccup, the comparison that finally cracked it.</p>
<p><em>Measured, same machine, same AP, minutes apart:</em></p>
<ul>
<li>6 GHz primary — stalled in <em>21 seconds</em></li>
<li>2.4 GHz — clean for 11 minutes</li>
<li>5 GHz anchored — over an hour, and survived a suspend/resume, which had been a reliable trigger.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>The fix.</em> Pin <code>802-11-wireless.bssid</code> to a 5 GHz BSSID so it can never anchor
on 6 GHz. MLO still attaches 6 GHz as link 2 and that is harmless; what matters
is which band anchors the association. Costs roaming — the machine stays on one unit.</p>
<p>Here's the <a href="https://gist.github.com/jackbaty/6b03995706e516fc9fadd7e9ff9d0b04">wifi-pin.sh script</a>.</p>
